A||||7||fropuff||Can I do this?||||10||
Z||000000||fropuff||05-23-2001||05:16 PM||||Hey.  Since I've decided to go with red ground FX, I have also decided to paint the wheels black.  I have the GT lace wheels, and they are currently silver.  I am wondering if I can just scuff up the silver paint and repaint the wheels black?  Or do I need to use a stripper and sand?  There is no damage to the wheels, so I wouldn't need to repair anything.  Any help is appreciated.  Thanks a lot!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters rock!||||reg||10
Z||000001||spideyfoot||05-23-2001||05:26 PM||||Boy if you could find somewhere to powdercoat them black you would be set.||||reg||1
Z||000002||pontiacs4me||05-23-2001||08:46 PM||||Powdercoat is definately the way to go.  Paint will just eventually chip off from driving.  Powdercoat will last a real long time.  There is a company here that will blast your wheels down and powdercoat them any color you want, for 40 bucks each.  You can ship 2 wheels at a time pretty cheap (UPS) probably 10 bucks for 2, so 20 bucks, each way.  They look really good also.  My moms boyfriend had his jeep rims done, and they are awesome.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||05-23-2001||11:41 PM||||Well, I agree that this is the thing to do, but it would only be a temporary thing.  I plan to get some 16" & 17" chromers next year.  This will only be for a few months.  So, can I do it?  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||05-23-2001||11:46 PM||||Why not just trade someone for their black wheels?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jefrysuko||05-24-2001||02:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>Powdercoat is definately the way to go.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You do realize that the original finish was a powdercoating right?  That is why they last so long in the first place!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>Why not just trade someone for their black wheels?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Being that those would be originally off an 88 they have a different offset which looks weird on a prev model and are quite a bit more rare than grey lace and thus hard to find someone interested in a direct trade.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fropuff:<BR><B>This will only be for a few months.  So, can I do it?  Thanks!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No offense but since its only going to be a few months why not just wait?  I am sure that in a couple months when you are ready to buy your rims someone here would be willing to buy yours.  That is if you don't change them.<P>As for an answer to your question.  Since the original finish is powdercoating simply scuffing them up wouldn't produce a surface sutiable for painting.  Regular stripper won't have the normal effect that it has on paint either.  Don't get me wrong stripper will ruin a powdercoated surface but it is very hard to clean it all up.  I would say that you would have to sand them down to a good rough surface and prime and paint.<P>------------------<BR>Jeffrey D Suckow<BR>88 GT<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:Jefrysuko@aol.com">Jefrysuko@aol.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fropuff||05-24-2001||08:06 AM||||Well, maybe I'll just leave em.  I just thought that monochromatic red with silver wheels would look weird.  Anyone have a pic of a monochromatic fastback GT with silver lace wheels?  Thanks!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters rock!||||reg||10
Z||000007||LZeitgeist||05-24-2001||08:11 AM||||Heck, I think red with silver lace rims would *rock*!!!!||||reg||1
